  "name": "Question 18",
  "problem": "A particle $P$ moves in the $x$-$y$ plane (relative to a fixed origin $O$) with position vector\n\n$$\nr=\u27e8\\operatorname{ln}\u2061((t-1)^{2}+2),(t-1)^{3}\u27e9,\\,t\\in [0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate the time at which the particle is stationary. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe particle is stationary at $t=$\n{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061((t-1)^{2}+2)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t-1)^{3})\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{2(t-1)}{(t-1)^{2}+2},\\,3(t-1)^{2}\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when its velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ \u27e8\\frac{2(t-1)}{(t-1)^{2}+2},\\,3(t-1)^{2}\u27e9 & =\u27e80,0\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2(t-1)}{(t-1)^{2}+2}=0\\, & \u21d2\\,t=1, \\\\ 3(t-1)^{2}=0\\, & \u21d2\\,t=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution $t=1$ is the only solution that satisfies both equations, so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
